{{> Top frame}}
<header>
  <p class="kicker">Go deeper</p>
  <h1>Ship</h1>
  <p class="lede">One executable, its static files beside it. No runtime to install, no proxy in front, no container.</p>
</header>

<section class="turn-box">
  <h2>Your turn</h2>
  <pre class="term"><code>./roux build main.roc
ls -lh main</code></pre>
  <p>About 3 MB: the whole app, optimized. The server, your Roc, the templates' bytecode,
  SQLite. Now run it somewhere else, as a server would, with its static files beside it:</p>
  <pre class="term"><code>mkdir ../shipped &amp;&amp; cp -r main static ../shipped
cd ../shipped &amp;&amp; ROUX_PORT=8081 ./main</code></pre>
  <p>Open <a href="http://localhost:8081">localhost:8081</a>. It makes its own
  <code>tutor.db</code> there: an empty guestbook, as on a new server. (This tab's app holds
  the one in your clone.)</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>On a server</h2>
  <p>Copy <code>main</code> and <code>static/</code> to any machine with Linux 6.7 or later
  and an x86-64-v3 CPU, and run it. For HTTPS (kernel TLS: <code>modprobe tls</code> where it
  is a module) it gets its own certificate from Let's Encrypt at start, and redirects plain
  HTTP:</p>
  <pre class="term"><code>ROUX_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0 ROUX_PORT=443 ROUX_REDIRECT_PORT=80 \
ROUX_ACME_DIRECTORY=https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory \
ROUX_ACME_IDENTIFIER=example.com ROUX_ACME_STATE=/var/lib/myapp \
./main</code></pre>
  <p>Over HTTPS it speaks HTTP/2, so a browser's requests and event streams share one
  connection: live pages in many tabs do not run out of the six connections HTTP/1.1 allows
  a site.</p>
  <p>The server is <a href="https://github.com/Eldhus/fourneau">fourneau</a>, written in Zig
  for exactly this: io_uring, a thread per core, your handlers on fibers. It races Go, Rust and
  others on nights something changed; the results are on a site that is itself a roux app:
  <a href="https://fourneau.y2kbugger.com/">fourneau.y2kbugger.com</a>.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Where next</h2>
